# Line-ending policy.
#
# Two classes of file, deliberately treated differently:
#
#   design sources     normalised to LF in the repository AND in the working
#                      tree, so a checkout is byte-identical on every platform
#                      and a frozen-fixture digest means something.
#
#   production output  never touched. Gerbers, drills, the fabrication archive
#                      and the BOM/CPL that ship to a fabricator are hashed as
#                      exact raw bytes; normalising them after generation would
#                      invalidate the hashes recorded in the release manifest.
#
# This policy is carried over unchanged from PCB_MicrophoneArrayV2. The live
# board's approved waivers are bound to the SHA-256 of the native board and
# project files, so any change here would silently unbind them.

# Default: text, normalised to LF in the repo and on checkout.
*                    text=auto eol=lf

# --- KiCad design files: text, checked out CRLF ---------------------------
#
# `eol=crlf` rather than `eol=lf`, and it has to be exactly this. Two committed
# things bind digests of these files, and they bind DIFFERENT forms:
#
#   the approved DRC waivers in board/manifest.live.json record
#   approved_source_sha256 / approved_rules_sha256 over the file's RAW bytes,
#   and were reviewed against the CRLF form (51,958 CRLF pairs in the board);
#
#   the committed check reports under generated/release/reports/ bind the
#   CANONICAL digest, which pcbqa.canonical computes over LF bytes so that it
#   survives a checkout on any platform.
#
# So a checkout must produce CRLF bytes while the file still classifies as
# text. `text eol=crlf` does exactly that: the blob is stored LF, the working
# tree gets CRLF, and AttributePolicy.classify sees `text` and canonicalises to
# LF. Both digests match.
#
# The two failure modes are not hypothetical - a fresh clone was made and both
# were observed:
#
#   `text eol=lf`  checks out LF. Raw digest changes, both waivers stop
#                  matching, and two reviewed findings block again.
#   `-text`        preserves CRLF but classifies as binary, so the canonical
#                  digest becomes the raw one and both committed reports stop
#                  being tied to their sources.
*.kicad_pcb          text eol=crlf
*.kicad_sch          text eol=crlf
*.kicad_pro          text eol=crlf
*.kicad_prl          text eol=crlf
*.kicad_sym          text eol=crlf
*.kicad_mod          text eol=crlf
*.kicad_dru          text eol=crlf
*.kicad_wks          text eol=crlf

# --- Project sources: explicit LF -----------------------------------------
fp-lib-table         text eol=lf
sym-lib-table        text eol=lf
*.py                 text eol=lf
*.md                 text eol=lf
*.json               text eol=lf
*.net                text eol=lf
*.csv                text eol=lf

# --- Production / fabrication output: exact bytes, never normalised -------
# Anything under a release directory is treated as binary so Git performs no
# EOL translation in either direction.
generated/release/** -text

# --- Frozen upstream evidence: exact bytes --------------------------------
# The SHA-256 of these response bodies is the evidence. They happen to contain
# no line endings at all today, so normalisation would be a no-op - but the
# point is that Git is never in a position to touch them.
fabrication/jlc_orientation/raw/** -text

*.gbr                binary
*.gbrjob             binary
*.drl                binary
*.zip                binary
*.pdf                binary
*.png                binary
*.jpg                binary
*.step               binary
*.stp                binary
*.wrl                binary
